+# Decision: Concurrency Policy for Competing Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+A visit can be edited independently by more than one field worker before either edit is reviewed — workers compare field notes days apart, and a tablet can hold a correction offline for up to 48 hours before it reaches the server. When two edits start from the same prior state, the record store must decide what becomes of the visit: whether the edits are combined, whether one displaces the other, or whether both are held for a coordinator to judge. Getting this wrong either loses a worker's correction or produces an exported value a coordinator never actually approved — in a system whose only check against bad data is coordinator review, that is the central risk the record store exists to manage.
+
+A visit's scientific fields are not independent of each other: a species identification and its cover estimate, for instance, read differently together than either does alone. Any mechanism that acts on fields without understanding their scientific relationships can combine two edits into a record neither worker submitted and no coordinator saw as a whole.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Both options under consideration already satisfy the standing constraint that no submitted edit may be discarded — persistence keeps every original submission regardless of which policy is chosen. That invariant is background to this decision, not a differentiator between the options, so it is not scored below.
+
+**Weighted criteria:**
+
+- **Risk of silently crossing a scientific relationship** (major) — does the mechanism ever produce a record a worker didn't submit and a coordinator didn't see, by combining fields it cannot tell are related? Derives from the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*: an exported value must trace back to a submission a human actually judged, not to an inferred combination.
+- **Review workload generated** (major) — how much coordinator attention does ordinary concurrent editing generate, independent of whether the edits are scientifically related?
+- **Operability without a dedicated systems administrator** (moderate) — does the mechanism need an ongoing, maintained ruleset (per-field-type combination logic) that someone has to keep correct as the schema grows, or does it need no such ruleset? Derives from the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Preservation of whole-visit intent** (moderate) — does a stored or exported record remain exactly what one worker submitted, or can it become a composite no single worker ever saw end to end?
+- **Comprehensibility of pending state to field workers** (minor) — can tablet-sync describe a worker's own submission status in simple terms ("your edit is current" / "your edit is pending review"), or does the worker's view of their own data become partial/split?
+
+No gate eliminates an option outright here — the real force in this decision is the major criteria pulling in opposite directions, not a disqualifying constraint.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission names the revision it started from. A submission based on the current head becomes the new head outright. A submission based on a stale revision becomes a competing revision stored alongside the current head; the visit has no head until a coordinator picks one of the competing revisions to become it. No submission is ever algorithmically combined with another — a coordinator always resolves a competition by selecting, never by merging.
+
+- Scientific-relationship risk: **strong**. No two submissions are ever combined by the system, so no relationship the system can't see is ever crossed.
+- Review workload: **weak**. Any two concurrent edits to the same visit create review work, even when they touch entirely unrelated fields — e.g., one worker fixing an observer's name and another adding a missing species, from the same base, both need a coordinator's decision under this policy even though a human would see at a glance that they don't conflict.
+- Administrator independence: **strong**. There is no per-field combination logic to build or maintain.
+- Preservation of whole-visit intent: **strong**. Every stored revision, including the head, is exactly one worker's submitted payload.
+- Worker-facing comprehensibility: **strong**. A worker's own submission is either the current head or a pending competing revision — a binary a tablet can state plainly.
+
+**Summary**: trades a larger, less-targeted review queue for zero risk of an unseen scientific combination and no merge logic to maintain.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission supplies its base state and its changes against that base. Two submissions from the same base are combined automatically when their changed fields are disjoint; overlapping field changes are held for coordinator review. Review-export would need field-level provenance to show a coordinator (and later, a reader of the exported record) which submission contributed which field.
+
+- Scientific-relationship risk: **weak**. Disjoint-field combination is exactly the case that can silently cross a scientific relationship the merge rules don't know about — the species-identification/cover-estimate pair is the canonical failure: two submissions that look field-disjoint (one touches identification, the other touches cover) would auto-combine into a record that doesn't necessarily describe one coherent observation.
+- Review workload: **strong**. Only genuinely overlapping edits reach a coordinator; the observer-name/missing-species example above would combine automatically and never reach review.
+- Administrator independence: **weak**. Combination rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries need to be built and kept correct as the schema evolves — a form of ongoing engineering maintenance, not a one-time decision.
+- Preservation of whole-visit intent: **weak**. A visit's current state can be a composite stitched from two submissions that no single worker ever reviewed as a whole.
+- Worker-facing comprehensibility: **partial**. A worker's own edit may show as partially applied, blended with another worker's unrelated change, which is harder for tablet-sync to state plainly than a binary current/pending status.
+
+**Summary**: trades a smaller, more-targeted review queue for merge logic that must be built and kept correct, and a standing risk of silently crossing a scientific relationship the rules don't model.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The major criterion that decides this is scientific-relationship risk, weighed against the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*. Field-aware merging's central appeal — combining disjoint-looking edits automatically — is also its central failure mode here: "disjoint" is a property of field names, not of scientific meaning, and the record store has no way to tell the two apart. Whole-visit revisions forecloses that failure entirely by never combining submissions at all; the cost is accepted explicitly: review workload scales with the rate of concurrent editing, not with the rate of genuine scientific conflict, so unrelated-field edits generate exactly as much review work as related-field ones. The *operability without a dedicated systems administrator* tenet reinforces the same choice from a different angle — merge rules are a maintained artifact the project would otherwise be committing to keep correct as the species list and record schema grow.
+
+This also closes the loop opened above: a reader might ask why the team doesn't just scope field-aware merging down to a short list of fields known to be scientifically independent (e.g., observer-list typos) rather than rejecting it wholesale. That remains a live option *in principle*, but it was not chosen because it reopens exactly the administrator-independence cost (a maintained, if smaller, ruleset) for a review-workload saving that can't be sized yet — it would need field-by-field evidence from the review queue itself that a specific class of edits never carries scientific risk, which doesn't exist before the system has run.
+
+**What this forecloses:** automatic combination of any two submissions, under any field-independence rule, is off the table unless this decision is revisited — the record store will not gain merge logic as a later optimization without a new decision here, since the risk the current choice avoids is exactly what merge logic reintroduces.
+
+**What would make field-aware merging worth reconsidering:** if the whole-visit review queue grows faster than two coordinators can clear it, and the backlog is dominated by edits to clearly unrelated fields, that would be new evidence about the actual cost this decision accepted — at that point the smaller, evidence-backed version of field-aware merging (a short, reviewed list of fields with no known scientific relationship to any other field) would be the one worth bringing back, not the general mechanism rejected here.
+
+**What tablet-sync must accommodate:**
+- Retain the base revision ID for each local draft, so a submission (or a worker's later correction) names the revision it actually started from.
+- Carry an idempotency key per submission so a connection failure during the up-to-48-hour offline window doesn't create a duplicate revision on retry.
+- Represent a worker's own submission status as current-head or pending-review in plain terms, without implying the server rejected or lost the work — the response to a submission may not arrive until a later sync.
+- Handle the case where a worker's base revision goes stale before their correction reaches the server (another edit, or a resolution, landed first) by surfacing that plainly rather than as a generic error.
+
+**What review-export must accommodate:**
+- A revision-comparison view for coordinators that presents competing revisions side by side for selection, not a diff-and-merge tool — resolution is always a choice among existing submissions, never an edit.
+- Surfacing of the resolution outcome (coordinator identity and the full set of competing submission IDs) for inspectability, per the record store's resolution contract.
+- An export-eligibility rule of "has a head" — any visit with unresolved competing revisions is excluded from the Friday CSV regardless of which fields its competing edits touch.
+- A coordinator review queue sized for concurrent-edit volume generally, not just genuine scientific conflicts — the queue will include visits whose competing edits don't actually overlap in meaning.
